WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.835 --> 00:00:04.921
My name is Tumeletso Setlabosha. 

00:00:05.296 --> 00:00:07.257
But people call me…

00:00:08.133 --> 00:00:09.718
Water. 

00:00:21.938 --> 00:00:25.567
I live in the center of the Okavango Delta.

00:00:26.192 --> 00:00:28.028
It's wonderful.

00:00:31.865 --> 00:00:34.034
As a young man, I was a tracker,

00:00:34.034 --> 00:00:36.119
helping people to hunt wildlife. 

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:40.331
Elephant footprint. It came from this way.

00:00:43.001 --> 00:00:44.836
Five Zebras!

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:51.134
But now I use my skills as a guide. 

00:00:53.261 --> 00:00:56.014
The Okavango Delta is protected, 

00:00:56.014 --> 00:00:58.308
and people in Botswana can benefit from

00:00:58.308 --> 00:01:00.643
a conservation-based tourism economy.

00:01:04.898 --> 00:01:06.399
Our communities thrive because people come

00:01:06.399 --> 00:01:08.651
from all over the world to see our wildlife. 

00:01:14.407 --> 00:01:16.284
Mr. Water belongs to one of 

00:01:16.284 --> 00:01:18.578
many communities who rely on the water

00:01:18.578 --> 00:01:22.832
that begins thousands of kilometers north in Angola. 

00:01:23.249 --> 00:01:24.250
But upstream, 

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:25.627
local people don’t benefit from

00:01:25.627 --> 00:01:28.338
the same sustainable livelihoods.

00:01:33.301 --> 00:01:37.013
There used to be a lot of animals here before… 

00:01:37.013 --> 00:01:41.893
elephants, lions, rhinos… 

00:01:43.269 --> 00:01:48.233
Now hunting has become a vice in this territory. 

00:01:49.317 --> 00:01:50.652
Like Mr. Water, 

00:01:50.652 --> 00:01:54.197
Elias lives from what the Okavango Basin provides. 

00:01:54.614 --> 00:01:57.534
But overhunting, deforestation

00:01:57.534 --> 00:02:00.161
and unchecked commercial agriculture

00:02:00.161 --> 00:02:02.956
are putting the source waters of the Delta at risk, 

00:02:02.956 --> 00:02:05.792
leaving not only Elias’ livelihood at stake, 

00:02:05.792 --> 00:02:08.586
but also a million other people living across 

00:02:08.586 --> 00:02:10.964
Angola, Namibia, and Botswana.

00:02:11.464 --> 00:02:14.551
We’d prefer to stop hunting

00:02:14.551 --> 00:02:19.430
so as not to destroy the lives of living things. 

00:02:26.271 --> 00:02:29.274
Working with Elias, Mr.  Water,

00:02:29.274 --> 00:02:32.068
and many others across the region, 

00:02:32.068 --> 00:02:33.653
National Geographic and De Beers

00:02:33.653 --> 00:02:36.865
through their Okavango Eternal partnership

00:02:36.865 --> 00:02:39.784
will support community-based conservation 

00:02:39.784 --> 00:02:43.538
and help ensure that land is protected.

00:02:43.538 --> 00:02:46.624
Cooperatives, local community training,

00:02:46.624 --> 00:02:49.335
and support for sustainable agriculture  

00:02:49.335 --> 00:02:51.546
and small business development 

00:02:51.546 --> 00:02:54.757
all contribute to local communities prospering 

00:02:54.757 --> 00:02:56.885
in harmony with nature

00:02:58.803 --> 00:03:01.139
as their stewardship safeguards

00:03:01.139 --> 00:03:04.225
the future of the entire Okavango System. 

